last":2025,"intercept":-383.64376998890395,"slope":0.2295643347310092,"total":29.2},"snow_normal":77.8},"titles":{"year":"2026 so far: 6 daily record highs, 1 daily record low","records":"Grand Rapids has nearly stopped setting record lows","change":"Grand Rapids' nights have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","rain":"Grand Rapids has gotten wetter"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 41\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>41\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 48\u00b0F, and the record is 70\u00b0F (1989). The low of <strong>32\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Grand Rapids has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Grand Rapids' climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Grand Rapids has set <strong class=\"sig\">25 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 74 that chance alone would give, and <strong>86 record highs</strong>, close to the 74 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (50). Grand Rapids' all-time high is 108\u00b0F, on July 13, 1936.","t_chg":"Grand Rapids' nights have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 130 complete years: Grand Rapids' average low shows no clear change: +0.04\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.04 to +0.13) that includes zero. Grand Rapids' average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.09\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.02 to +0.17), about +1.2\u00b0F since 1894. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.80\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.39 to +1.20) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.63\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.19 to +1.07). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 1 of 12 months. Highs rose in 2 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Grand Rapids has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Grand Rapids' yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.75 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.48 to +1.02), about +9.7 inches since 1896. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 39.4 inches a year. The wettest year was 2019 (51.37 in); the driest, 1930 (20.92 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 6 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}